Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 14:45:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH libnsfb 0/2] x surface: work on X servers without MIT-SHM

Hi,

These two patches fix the xcb surface on X servers that lack the
MIT-SHM extension, such as a server started with -extension MIT-SHM.
Today the framebuffer exits almost immediately on such a server, even
though the non-shm path in update_pixmap() exists for exactly this
case. It never gets reached because of two xcb behaviours that differ
from Xlib:

1. xcb closes the connection when a request is issued for an extension
   the server does not have, so create_shm_image()'s NULL-reply check
   comes too late. Patch 1 asks xcb_get_extension_data() first.

2. xcb_put_image() does not split images larger than the maximum
   request length the way XPutImage does. Without BIG-REQUESTS that is
   256 KiB, and a full redraw of a 760x500 32bpp window is about
   1.5 MB, so the next full update closes the connection. Patch 2 sends
   the update in full-width bands that fit.

Servers with MIT-SHM are unaffected: patch 1 takes the same path as
before, and patch 2 only touches the non-shm branch.

I found these running NetSurf's framebuffer frontend with the x surface
against a small core-protocol X server (no extensions) inside
LinuxOnTab, a Linux-in-the-browser project. Both patches were built
against current libnsfb master (b701cdc) and tested there. Browsing
works, including full-window redraws. With patch 1 the surface prints
"Server has no shm support." and continues on the put-image path.

Thanks,
Kilian

kilian-ai (2):
  x surface: check for MIT-SHM before querying it
  x surface: split non-shm image updates to fit the request length

 src/surface/x.c | 59 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----------
